<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuremberg trials</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as International Military Tribunal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials were international criminal trials held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> against leaders of defeated Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for invasions and atrocities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Between 20 November 1945 and 1 October 1946 the International Military Tribunal tried 22 of the most important surviving Nazi leaders and six German organisations; twelve defendants, among them Göring, Ribbentrop and Keitel, were sentenced to death. The United States held twelve further trials of lower-level perpetrators. Controversial for criminalising aggression retroactively, the trials are considered the beginning of international criminal law.</p></div>
